  q(NEURO,'Which scale is used to score eye opening, verbal and motor responses after a head injury?','Glasgow Coma Scale',['Braden Scale','Borg scale','Berg Balance Scale'],'D','Scores range from 3 to 15, with 8 or below meaning severe brain injury.',PAG),
  q(CP,'Using the Karvonen formula, what is the 60% target heart rate for a 40-year-old with a resting rate of 70?','136 beats per minute',['108 beats per minute','180 beats per minute','96 beats per minute'],'A','Heart rate reserve = (220 − 40) − 70 = 110; 110 × 0.6 + 70 = 136.',PF),
  q(CP,'Which condition is a reason not to start exercise that day?','Unstable angina, or chest pain at rest',['Mild muscle soreness from yesterday','A resting heart rate of 72','A blood pressure of 118/76'],'B','Unstable angina may signal a coming heart attack and needs medical care.',PF),
  q(CP,'A patient with peripheral artery disease has calf pain when walking that goes away with rest. What is this called?','Intermittent claudication',['Deep vein thrombosis','Angina','Orthopnea'],'C','Walking programs to moderate pain, then resting, can improve walking distance.',PF),
  q(CP,'A patient with heart failure gains 3 pounds in one day. What should the PT do?','Report it, since it may mean fluid build-up',['Ignore it, since weight always changes','Increase exercise intensity right away','Tell the patient to stop drinking all fluids'],'D','Daily weights are an early warning sign of worsening heart failure.',PF),
  q(CP,'Which treatment is used for lymphedema after breast cancer surgery?','Complete decongestive therapy, including manual lymph drainage and compression',['Heat packs on the swollen arm several times a day to open the blood vessels and drain the fluid','Complete rest of the arm','Blood pressure checks on the affected arm only'],'A','Skin care and exercise are also part of managing lymphedema, which is often lifelong.',PF),
  q(CP,'Why is diaphragmatic breathing taught to patients with lung disease?','It improves breathing efficiency and reduces use of neck muscles',['It increases the work of breathing','It is used only to raise blood pressure in patients who feel dizzy when standing up quickly','It replaces all other therapy'],'B','The abdomen rises with each breath while the chest stays fairly still.',PF),
  q(CP,'An ankle-brachial index (ABI) below 0.9 suggests which condition?','Peripheral artery disease',['Normal circulation','Heart failure','Asthma'],'C','The ABI compares systolic blood pressure at the ankle with the arm; about 1.0 to 1.4 is normal.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'How is a stage 1 pressure injury described?','Intact skin with redness that does not fade when pressed',['An open wound showing bone','A blister filled with clear fluid that has broken open over the heel or tailbone','Black eschar covering the wound'],'D','Stage 1 is the earliest sign, and relieving pressure can prevent further damage.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Intact skin that is purple or maroon over a bony area suggests which injury?','A deep tissue pressure injury',['A stage 1 pressure injury','A healed scar','A superficial burn'],'A','The damage is under the skin and may quickly open into a deep wound.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Which burn affects only the epidermis, is red and painful, and has no blisters?','Superficial burn',['Partial-thickness burn','Full-thickness burn','Fourth-degree burn into muscle and bone'],'B','A sunburn is a common example and usually heals in a few days without scars.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'After a burn to the armpit, how is the arm usually positioned to prevent a contracture?','Shoulder abducted to about 90 degrees',['Arm held tightly against the side','Elbow bent fully','Hand in a fist'],'C','Burns tend to contract into the position of comfort, so splinting stretches them out.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Skin around a wound is white, soft and wrinkled. What is this called?','Maceration',['Eschar','Granulation','Erythema'],'D','Maceration comes from too much moisture and can make the wound bigger.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'Wet-to-dry dressings are an example of which type of debridement?','Nonselective mechanical debridement',['Autolytic debridement','Enzymatic debridement using an applied ointment','Sharp debridement'],'A','They remove both dead and healthy tissue, so they are used less often today.',PF),
  q(INTEG,'How does undermining differ from tunneling in a wound?','Undermining is tissue loss under the wound edge, while tunneling is a narrow channel',['They are the same thing, and the two words can be used interchangeably in any wound note','Undermining is always deeper than bone','Tunneling occurs only on the skin surface'],'B','Both are measured with a probe and described using clock positions.',PF),
  q(AGE,'By about what age does the palmar grasp reflex usually fade?','4 to 6 months',['2 weeks','About 2 years, when the child begins to run','10 years'],'C','Primitive reflexes that last too long may suggest a nervous system problem.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'A newborn’s arm hangs limp with the wrist bent after a hard delivery. What is likely?','Erb’s palsy (brachial plexus birth injury)',['Clubfoot','Torticollis from a tight neck muscle','Hip dysplasia'],'D','Injury to the C5–C6 nerve roots causes the “waiter’s tip” arm position.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Which condition causes brittle bones in children, so therapists must handle them very gently?','Osteogenesis imperfecta',['Down syndrome','Duchenne muscular dystrophy','Cerebral palsy'],'A','Children with this condition may break bones with very little force.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'A 6-year-old has a limp and hip pain from loss of blood supply to the femoral head. What is likely?','Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease',['Osgood-Schlatter disease','Scoliosis','Juvenile idiopathic arthritis'],'B','Treatment aims to keep the femoral head round as it heals, often with bracing and activity limits.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Which child has trouble with motor coordination that affects daily tasks without another medical cause?','Developmental coordination disorder',['Spina bifida','Down syndrome with low muscle tone','Hip dysplasia'],'C','Children may struggle with handwriting, catching balls and riding a bike.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Which CDC STEADI test has an older adult stand in four positions of increasing difficulty?','The 4-Stage Balance Test',['The Timed Up and Go','The 30-second chair stand','The 6-minute walk test'],'D','Not being able to hold a tandem stance for 10 seconds suggests higher fall risk.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'How should a PT give instructions to a patient with moderate dementia?','Use short, simple, one-step directions',['Give all steps at once in writing','Speak quickly and loudly','Avoid speaking to the patient'],'A','Demonstration and consistent routines also help patients with memory loss.',PAG),
  q(AGE,'Why does taking many medications (polypharmacy) raise fall risk in older adults?','Side effects such as dizziness and drowsiness can add up',['Medications always improve balance','Pills make bones stronger and less likely to break, so they prevent all falls','It has no effect on falls'],'B','Taking four or more medications is a common fall risk factor reviewed in screenings.',PAG)
